# A committed TEMPLATE of .github/c2-serving-job.env for the tp4/next-1 windows (the ship-5 candidate; scripts/ci/c2_serving_job.py parses it): copy it over
# .github/c2-serving-job.env on a throwaway commit of tp4/next-1 and push a tag experiment/c2-serving-vN, ONE TAG AT A TIME (wait for the run to finish; never cancel a running card job).
# Nothing here names a rig, a card, a host or a registry: the cards are resolved at run time. The jobs are templates only: no tag is pushed from the branch by its author. ORDER.txt, ORDER-W2.txt and
# ORDER-SHIP.txt have the order, the stop rules, the minutes and the read rules of the three runs.
# ONE IMAGE FOR THE WHOLE WINDOW: C2_IMAGE_TAG=tp4-next-1 is a PLACEHOLDER for the tag B0 builds from the pushed branch head WITH the K64j-OQ graft (sed -i 's/tp4-next-1/<tag>/' *.env). A commit of a file
# the image carries needs a new B0 build and a new tag in every template. This window never stops, starts or hands back the node agent.
# C2_MODEL_REPO / C2_MODEL_REVISION (qwen_c2_checkpoint.py): a job that carries them serves the Swift checkpoint (what production serves) from its own converted-weight directory and, since NP9, with the output
# ceiling of its catalog entry; the keys are the same in every smoke and prefix job of this pack (the prefix gate serves the selection in its containers too and fails an arm whose log lacks the checkpoint lines), so each pair
# differs by the profile alone. The replay and build steps do not forward the selection (the job reader refuses the keys beside them): they run the base checkpoint.
# N3 (NP9's N3 on the candidate): EIGHT requests at once, prompts of about 96,000 tokens (as the server counts them), 30,000 tokens each (ignore_eos), on Swift. The longest
# prompt at which eight 30,000-token requests are all resident: 8 x 1,971 = 15,768 of the 16,499 blocks (docs/tp4-np9-answer32k.md), so no seat waits: the container log
# holds NO "kv reservation hold" line (a hold here means the pool arithmetic is wrong). The eight prefills queue (about 26 s each), then eight seats decode at depth 96,000
# to 126,000 together for 30,000 tokens. CEILING READ: the container log holds "[QWEN-C2] request output budget 32768 for this checkpoint (its catalog entry; the profile
# says 16384)" and "[QWEN-C2] request contract installed: greedy, max_tokens <= 32768" (c2_smoke_check requires the second whenever a long-answer test ran), the checkpoint
# lines (c2_smoke_check --expect-checkpoint) and no "kv reservation too large" line. ANSWER READ (c2_smoke_check, long_answer_problems): every user of a long_answer test
# ends with finish "length" and exactly 30,000 tokens (ignore_eos, so the stop is the ceiling, not the model); fewer tokens at "length" is a server whose ceiling clamped
# the request. MEMORY READ: the output ceiling allocates no verifier capture, proposal bucket or page table (docs/tp4-np9-answer32k.md), so the DRAM lines are the
# candidate's own: no "dram backstop refused", no "dram admission hold" that the same prompts would not have drawn at 16,384, no engine-build failure, no "audit mismatch",
# no stall. READ also: the per-seat rate at depth is recorded against concurrent8_code_128k_long of the Swift window, never gated. RUN WITH THE CI PAUSED: the ARC runner
# sets are at ZERO for this job (ORDER, ARC-ZERO); no build, no CI push, no other job beside it. TELEMETRY (C2_TELEMETRY=1, scripts/ci/card_telemetry.py): the read-only ARC
# sidecar samples every chip once a second through the smoke step; the first [TELEMETRY] line must say decode check ok. A rate difference that comes with a different loaded
# AICLK between the arms is the clocks, not the lever. Duration (estimate): 80 minutes.
C2_CARDS=quad
C2_ACTIONS=reset smoke
C2_IMAGE_TAG=tp4-next-1
C2_PROFILE=c2-packed-tp4-8x262k-ship-prefix-levern-w2-er-fx-traffic-s5
C2_MODEL_REPO=ukisai/Swift-1.5-Qwen3.8-27b
C2_MODEL_REVISION=b4c84d42903a8646b25857eb2827288d92ed85a4
C2_SMOKE_TESTS=warmup,concurrent8_steady,long_answer8_96k
C2_TELEMETRY=1
C2_BOX_MINUTES=150
